WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.339
While the cast of Waco is universally great,
the actor tasked with portraying David Koresh

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.740
frequently steals the show.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:11.240
That actor is Taylor Kitsch, and while he's
all but unrecognizable in the role, you've

00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:13.170
absolutely seen his face before.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.940
As an actor, Kitsch has been around for a
couple of decades now, and has built an impressive

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:17.860
acting résumé.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.560
That résumé includes a memorable stint in
one of HBO's most critically adored shows.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.780
Unfortunately, that stint ended up coming
in the season of the series that critics liked

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:27.580
the least.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:32.500
The show in question is, of course, HBO's
anthology crime saga True Detective, which

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.190
premiered to rave reviews in the winter of
2014 behind the work of Matthew McConaughey,

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.340
Woody Harrelson, and series director Cary
Joji Fukunaga.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.580
Sadly, none of those talented individuals
returned for True Detective's second season.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.300
Instead, series creator Nic Pizzolatto conjured
up a new tale of cops and criminals for the

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.880
show's second run of episodes.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.660
In doing so, he also put together a seriously
impressive cast to bring those stories to

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:00.090
light, with Rachel McAdams, Colin Farrell,
Vince Vaughn, Kelly Reilly and more joining

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.060
Kitsch for the series second go-'round.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.040
It…could've gone better.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.100
"It's functionally impossible to give you
a synopsis of this season."

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.470
With such a stacked cast, season 2 may well
have provided too much of a good thing for

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.320
Pizzolatto and his writing team, as they struggled
through an underwhelming, tragically unfocused

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.140
run of episodes overstuffed with too many
characters and narrative entanglements.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.500
Though Kitsch's character, Paul Woodrugh,
was frequently at the center of the season's

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.950
melodrama, he was just as frequently among
its more interesting individuals, coping with

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.050
a treasure trove of personal anguish and inner
demons that would've been enough for an entire

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.320
season of television in and of themselves.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.170
Like many up-and-coming actors, Taylor Kitsch's
early years were spotted with supporting roles

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.380
opposite bigger names.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.680
Like many young actors, Taylor Kitsch was
once gifted a coveted role in an aspiring

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.200
blockbuster with full-on franchise potential.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.920
And, like so many young aspiring superstars
before him, things didn't quite work out.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.780
To be fair, that doesn't have much to do with
Kitsch, himself.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.420
The truth is, there are not many people in
or out of showbiz who could tell you exactly

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.510
what went wrong with Disney's 2012 tentpole,
John Carter.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:11.780
It had all the makings of a surefire, family-friendly,
crowd-pleasing hit…until it got released.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:17.150
When John Carter finally hit theaters in 2012,
it did so behind a middling critical response,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.850
and crippling word of mouth, with the film's
unintentional campiness most frequently mentioned

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.180
as its biggest problem.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.300
Not surprisingly, the film, which is nowhere
near as bad as you've heard, became one of

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.670
the biggest box office bombs in Disney history,
claiming just north of $73 million at U.S.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:37.280
box offices, and barely recouping its mammoth
budget with its worldwide take of just over

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.860
$284 million.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.010
Though it's quite likely you've seen Kitsch
in one of his big-screen roles over the years,

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.650
it's far more likely you recognize him from
his early days on television.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.780
Kitsch did, after all, feature prominently
in one of the best-loved dramas of the 2000s,

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.870
one that also proved small-screen adaptations
of big-screen successes aren't always doomed

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:57.480
to failure.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:03.180
Based on the 2004 film of the same name, the
TV show in question is NBC's rousing sports

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.770
drama, Friday Night Lights.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:08.849
If you somehow slept on the book, the movie,
and the series, Friday Night Lights follows

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:13.349
the travails of a coach and the players of
a powerhouse football program in a small Texas

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:13.840
town.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.500
Among Friday Night Lights' young cast were
future stars like Jesse Plemmons, Adrienne

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.349
Palicki, and Michael B. Jordan.

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:25.660
Taylor Kitsch also appeared in 68 of the show's
76 episodes as the roguish star running back

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.680
Tim Riggins.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.120
If we aren't being too bold, we'd say he was
frequently brilliant on the series, imbuing

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.680
his stoic character with a natural charisma
and hard-earned wisdom even as he was frequently

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.410
boozing it up and generally running amok all
over town.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:42.620
Of course, if you do recognize Kitsch from
one of his big-screen ventures, it's entirely

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:49.720
likely that venture came in his better-than-the-film
turn as Gambit in 2009's dreadful X-Men Origins:

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.300
Wolverine.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.740
If you happen to recognize Kitsch from another
movie, however, it may well be for his hilarious

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:59.370
appearance in the Samuel L. Jackson-starring,
horror-tinged camp-fest the world came to

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.220
know as Snakes on a Plane.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.200
If you managed to miss Kitsch in the film,
it's because he wasn't actually in Snakes

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.400
on a Plane for very long.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.440
In fact, his randy newlywed was one of the
first characters to die.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.780
Fortunately, it was far from the last we'd
see of Kitsch and, as his Waco performance

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.340
indicates, he has plenty more left in the
tank.

